Source: pve-meta
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Julius Arkenberg <julius@rkenberg.de>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13),
               libperl-dev
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Homepage: https://github.com/arki05/pve-meta

Package: pve-meta
Architecture: all
Depends: pve-manager (>= 9.0),
         pve-ext (>= 0.2.0),
         libpve-meta-rs-perl (= ${binary:Version}),
         init-system-helpers (>= 1.54~),
         ${misc:Depends}, ${perl:Depends}
Description: Structured, versioned metadata store for Proxmox VE
 pve-meta adds a structured YAML metadata document per guest, stored under /etc/pve/meta and replicated by pmxcfs like
 any other cluster file. The API is served natively by pveproxy/pvedaemon
 (PVE::API2::Ext::Meta, discovered by pve-ext, see docs/DESIGN.md) under
 /api2/json/meta/...; this package also ships the editor UI (a native ExtJS
 tab, as a pve-ext page manifest) and the guest-lifecycle patches that carry
 metadata through create, destroy, snapshot, rollback, backup and restore (as a
 pve-ext managed-patch manifest).

Package: libpve-meta-rs-perl
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, ${perl:Depends}
Description: pve-meta store bindings for PVE (Rust, perlmod)
 PVE::RS::Meta, a Perl binding (via perlmod) to pve-meta's guest metadata
 store: the lifecycle hooks called from libpve-guest-common-perl, the backup
 hooks called from qemu-server and pve-container, and the api_* functions
 behind PVE::API2::Ext::Meta.

Package: pve-meta-guest-files
Architecture: any
Depends: pve-meta (= ${source:Version}),
         pve-container,
         ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: Write views of pve-meta documents into LXC containers as files
 A per-node daemon that reads the guest-files key of each container's pve-meta
 document and writes the views it names into that container as files, one
 way, host to guest, so services inside read configuration managed in
 pve-meta. Writes are atomic and idempotent; a file edited inside the
 container, or one pve-meta-guest-files did not write, is left alone unless the
 entry says otherwise. Ships the guest-files prefix and a systemd unit.
